  "answer_text": "**Yes, Coca-Cola did originally contain cocaine.**\n\nWhen the beverage was first invented in 1885 by Atlanta pharmacist John Pemberton, cocaine was legal and commonly used in medicines, elixirs, and tonics. \n\nHere is the history of how cocaine got into Coca-Cola, how it was removed, and why the plant is still used in the drink today.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Original Formula and Name\nThe name **\"Coca-Cola\"** is a direct reference to its two main original ingredients:\n*   **\"Coca\"** came from the **coca leaf**, which naturally contains cocaine. Pemberton used a fluid extract of coca leaves in his syrup.\n*   **\"Cola\"** came from the **kola nut**, a West African fruit that naturally contains caffeine.\n\nAt the time, Pemberton marketed the drink as a patent medicine and a \"brain tonic\" capable of curing headaches, fatigue, and nervous disorders. He had actually been trying to create a non-alcoholic alternative to his previous product, \"Pemberton\u2019s French Wine Coca,\" due to local prohibition laws.\n\n### 2. How Much Cocaine Was in It?\nWhile the exact amounts are difficult to verify due to the secret nature of the formula, historical records suggest the amount of cocaine was relatively small. It was enough to provide a mild, refreshing stimulant effect\u2014comparable to a strong cup of coffee\u2014but likely not enough to trigger a massive, drug-induced \"high\". \n\n### 3. Why and When Was It Removed?\nBy the late 1890s and early 1900s, public perception of cocaine shifted drastically. As the public and medical community became aware of the drug's highly addictive nature and negative side effects, Coca-Cola\u2019s ownership (then led by Asa Candler) faced growing pressure. \n\nRather than remove the coca leaf entirely\u2014which would force them to change the highly valuable brand name \"Coca-Cola\"\u2014the company began reducing the drug's presence:\n*   **By 1903,** the company began using \"spent\" (decocainized) coca leaves, drastically reducing the cocaine content to mere traces.\n*   **By 1929,** advances in chemical extraction allowed the company to completely eliminate all active cocaine alkaloids from the flavoring.\n\n### 4. How Coca-Cola is Made Today\nTo this day, **Coca-Cola still uses coca leaves** to flavor the drink. Because of this, they are involved in a highly regulated, federally monitored supply chain:\n*   **The Stepan Company** in Maywood, New Jersey, is the only commercial entity in the United States legally authorized by the Drug Enforcement Administration (DEA) to import and process coca leaves.\n*   Stepan imports about 100 metric tons of dried coca leaves from South America every year. \n*   They process the leaves to extract the active cocaine, creating a **decocainized coca leaf extract**. This drug-free extract is then shipped directly to Coca-Cola to be used as a flavoring agent.\n*   The leftover active cocaine extracted from the leaves is sold to **Mallinckrodt**, a pharmaceutical company, which processes it into a legal local anesthetic used by ear, nose, throat (ENT) specialists and dentists.",
